[{"observable_id": "ddg-activation-regio-353k", "unit": "kcal/mol", "meaning": "Activation free-energy difference at 353 K between the two competing regioisomeric concerted Huisgen transition states of benzyl azide with methyl 4,4,4-trifluorobut-2-ynoate, reported as the higher-barrier (minor) orientation minus the lower-barrier (major) orientation, so its magnitude is the kinetic regioselectivity the synthetic decision turns on.", "role": "requested", "expected_sign": "positive", "expected_low": 0.0, "expected_high": 3.0, "expectation_basis": "Thermal azide-alkyne cycloadditions are documented to be only weakly regioselective, and here both alkyne termini carry acceptors that direct the terminal azide nitrogen in opposite senses, so the two asynchronous transition states should differ by tenths to a few kcal/mol rather than by many.", "required_tolerance": 0.5, "tolerance_basis": "The task's own decision rule: 'Anything below roughly 0.5 kcal/mol I will read as no thermal condition will fix this'.", "tolerance_origin": "task"}, {"observable_id": "dg-ts-esterc4-minus-esterc5-353k", "unit": "kcal/mol", "meaning": "Signed 353 K activation free energy of the transition state whose forming bonds deliver 1-benzyl-4-(methoxycarbonyl)-5-(trifluoromethyl)-1H-1,2,3-triazole (ester on IUPAC C4, the ring carbon bonded to N3) minus that of the transition state delivering the ester-on-C5 isomer; a negative value means the ester-at-C4 orientation is the thermal major product.", "role": "diagnostic", "method_resolution": 1.0, "expected_sign": "positive", "expected_low": 0.0, "expected_high": 2.5, "expectation_basis": "Frontier-orbital and electrostatic reading of the alkyne: the ester is a pi-acceptor and pushes LUMO density onto the distal CF3-bearing alkyne carbon, while CF3 acts mainly through sigma-induction and should not outweigh that resonance effect; the CF3-bearing carbon should therefore be both the larger-LUMO-coefficient and the more delta-positive terminus, so the nucleophilic terminal azide nitrogen (which becomes N3 and bonds C4) attacks it, placing CF3 at C4 and the ester at C5. This opposes the task's conventional 'ester to C4' expectation, so it is declared as a falsifiable diagnostic rather than assumed.", "failure_update_rule": "If the sign comes out negative outside the 1.0 kcal/mol deadband, the conventional ester direction controls instead: I would name the ester-at-C4 isomer as the thermal major product, revise the rationale to say the ester's own alkyne carbon is the preferred partner for the terminal azide nitrogen in the asynchronous transition state (or that benzyl-ester secondary interactions dominate the CF3 induction), and report that the user's target isomer is also the thermal major one."}, {"observable_id": "c4-restart-imaginary-mode-count", "unit": "1", "meaning": "Number of imaginary harmonic modes, under the 20 cm^-1 near-zero convention, of the ester-at-C4 saddle that the restarted search converges to from the geometry the cycle-2 search reached.", "role": "diagnostic", "expected_low": 1.0, "expected_high": 1.0, "expectation_basis": "The cycle-2 search was cut off by the iteration limit rather than by leaving the ridge: its reached structure still shows both forming N...C contacts at 2.07 and 2.27 A and its parsed mode list already carries an imaginary mode, so continuing the same walk from that structure should terminate on the same first-order saddle of the ester-at-C4 channel.", "failure_update_rule": "If the converged structure has zero imaginary modes it has fallen off the ridge into a minimum (a pi-complex or the triazole itself) and the ester-at-C4 barrier must be re-searched from a differently placed pre-saddle rather than reported; if it has two or more, the search found a higher-order saddle whose energy is not a barrier and cannot be used for the regiochemical comparison."}]

